WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.006 --> 00:00:12.242
THE SAGGING RAIL · SOLUTION
Right resistor, whole return path
Diagnosis (see "How to approach"): R2 is 1 kΩ instead of 10 kΩ, and D1's ground return is cut on the bottom layer.
Tools: Select (1) · Soldering iron (3) · Multimeter (2)

2
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.882
STEP 1 · REPLACE R2
Select R2 and fit 10 kΩ.

3
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:30.061
STEP 1 · CHECK
TP1 now reads +2.500 V.
Why: 5 V × 10k / (10k + 10k) = 2.50 V.

4
00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:39.328
STEP 2 · BRIDGE THE RETURN
On the bottom: the return trace is cut. Soldering iron (3): bridge it.

5
00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:46.270
STEP 3 · CHECK
D1 lights: its loop to ground is closed again.
Why: Current: (5 − ~3 V for blue) / 330 Ω ≈ 6 mA.

6
00:00:46.271 --> 00:01:01.706
STEP 4 · VERIFY
Run the test bench (Run tests).
Why: It powers the board, checks every objective, and only decrypts the flag if the circuit now matches the original design: a fix that merely works around a fault does not count.

7
00:01:01.708 --> 00:01:05.311
STEP 4 · VERIFY
All objectives pass and the flag is captured. Earn your own: it is blurred here.

8
00:01:05.312 --> 00:01:13.864
WHAT YOU LEARNED
Two layers, one loop
Swap a wrong resistor and confirm with the divider maths.
Bridge a broken trace on whichever layer it lives on.
